(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/scope-excluded-ops (vector :finalize-machine-setup-decision :authorize-machining-operation :proceed-with-machine-setup :override-safety-officer-judgment :override-shop-safety-officer-judgment)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/related (vector "2799007222-cloud-itonami-isco-7222-toolmakers-coverage" "2799007221-cloud-itonami-isco-7221-blacksmiths-hammersmiths-and-forging-press-workers-coverage")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "proceed with the machine setup" "proceed with the machining operation" "finalize the machine setup decision" "authorize the machining operation" "authorize the machine setup" "override the shop safety officer's judgment" "override the safety officer's judgment" "override shop safety officer judgment")), (:adr/id 2799007223), (:phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:flag-safety-concern :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}}}"), (:actor/domain :manufacturing/metal-working-machine-tool-coordination), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :worker-verified :workshop-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "7223"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007223: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7223 Metal Working Machine Tool Setters and Operators workshop scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7223\n-- Metal Working Machine Tool Setters and Operators** only, Wave3\n(production/trades) of the rollout (Wave0/1/2 100% complete, Wave3\nbatches 1-4 already landed: construction/metal trades 7111-7222,\nagriculture 6113/6129/6223/6224/6310/6320).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7223\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/\nget-occupation \\\"7223\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Metal Working Machine Tool\nSetters and Operators\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior\nto this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with\n`:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7223` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`7223` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nMetal Working Machine Tool Setters and Operators set up and operate\nCNC/manual machine tools (lathes, mills, presses) to shape and finish\nmetal parts -- real physical-safety stakes (rotating-machinery\nhazards, tooling-change injury, metal-shaving hazards). The\ncontrolling constraint for this vertical is that the closed op\nallowlist must NEVER include any op that directly finalizes a\nmachine-setup or machining-execution decision, or overrides a shop\nsafety officer's judgment -- those decisions must always be a hard\npermanent block or an always-escalate op, never auto-commit-eligible.\nThis actor is deliberately scoped to WORKSHOP SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS\nCOORDINATION ONLY -- it never performs machine setup or operation\nitself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7222`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7222)\n(Toolmakers: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor` split,\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set, the closely-related\nmachine-shop safety pattern already landed) -- cloned and read in full\nin this session.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7223` as an OSS workshop\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for metal working machine tool\nsetters and operators, following the SAME governed-actor architecture\nas every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Machinist\nAdvisor ⊣ MachinistGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **workshop scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never direct machine-setup or machining-execution authority** --\nit must NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising a machine-setup or machining-execution decision, or any\noverride of a shop safety officer's judgment. These are not merely\ngated behind escalation -- they are structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- task/batch/materials-usage/progress data\n  logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task/machine-time scheduling\n  proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a machinery-hazard/tool-condition\n  concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- tooling-materials procurement proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\nmachine-setup or machining-execution decision or a\nshop-safety-officer-judgment override. These are **structurally absent\nfrom the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation --\nan op such as `:finalize-machine-setup-decision` cannot even be\nproposed by the reference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for`\nfunctions only ever emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any\ncode path (a forged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving\nfork) ever produced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD\ncheck (`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A\nsecond, independent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names five\nconcretely-forbidden ops (`machinist.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-machine-setup-decision`, `:authorize-machining-operation`,\n`:proceed-with-machine-setup`, `:override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is\nverified by dedicated unit tests\n(`machinist.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-machine-setup-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-machining-operation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-machine-setup`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Worker unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered worker record (`:no-worker`). Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the worker is.\n2. **Workshop unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an\n   independently registered workshop (`:no-workshop`). Together with\n   check 1, this directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that a\n   worker/workshop record must be independently verified/registered\n   before any action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the five\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for proceeding with a machine\n   setup or machining operation, or overriding a shop safety officer's\n   judgment, is a second HARD, PERMANENT block\n   (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated unconditionally on content --\n   defense-in-depth on top of the allowlist check: even if a future\n   change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection still\n   independently blocks machine-setup/machining-execution/\n   safety-override language wherever it appears in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a machinery-hazard/\n  tool-condition observation -- it never resolves the concern itself,\n  and the robot's role ends at \"here is the hazard data,\" never \"here\n  is what I recommend doing about shop safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an\n  over-threshold tooling-materials order is not itself unsafe, unlike\n  a machine-setup/machining-execution or safety-officer-override\n  attempt, which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft\n  escalate vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"machine\", \"safety\") can accidentally match inside the mock advisor's\nown default rationale text for a legitimate proposal, self-blocking\nthe actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk this vertical's\nown vocabulary (a workshop coordination actor working adjacent to\nmachine setup/operation necessarily discusses those concepts to do its\nscheduling/logging job) could trigger if phrased carelessly.\n`machinist.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"proceed with the machine\nsetup\", \"authorize the machining operation\", \"override the shop\nsafety officer's judgment\" -- matching the task's own worked example\nphrasing), never bare nouns, and\n`machinist.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for worker ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for machine\ntool changeover ...\", \"... routed for shop safety officer review\")\nWITHOUT containing any of the full finalization-action phrases, so the\nregression test is a genuine stress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-\nphrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on first run in this\nsession -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never\ntripped during development. `machinist.actor-test/holds-a-scope-\nexcluded-op-even-at-high-confidence` additionally exercises the same\ninvariant through the full graph run, confirming a scope-excluded\nproposal never reaches `commit-record!` regardless of disposition\npath.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7223\" ...}` entry promoted\n  from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set to\n  the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7223` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7223`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 22 tests, 47 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-machine-setup` that is merely\n  gated behind an always-escalate list: rejected outright per the\n  task's explicit domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op\n  still means the *capability* to finalize a machine-setup or\n  machining-execution decision exists in the code path and is merely\n  deferred to a human gate. For a decision this severe\n  (rotating-machinery hazards, tooling-change injury,\n  metal-shaving hazards), the capability itself must not exist. The\n  only op related to shop-safety escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`,\n  which surfaces the need for human/shop-safety-officer attention and\n  never itself decides, authorizes, or defers a machine-setup or\n  machining-execution decision for later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the 3313\n  reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check): rejected per the\n  task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold tooling-materials\n  order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a soft escalate\n  gate, not a hard block; only the machine-setup/machining-execution/\n  safety-officer-override class is a hard, unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"machine\" \"lathe\"\n  \"safety\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked example for this\n  exact task), and per the explicit instruction to phrase\n  scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS, never bare\n  nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses\n  machine/tooling/safety-officer concepts to do its scheduling/logging\n  job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the actor's own\n  legitimate proposals (regression-tested directly against rationale\n  text that deliberately contains those bare nouns).\n- Two separate override-authority ops mirroring house-building's\n  safety-officer/foreman split: the task names only \"a shop safety\n  officer's judgment\" as the authority figure for this vertical, so a\n  single dominant op `:override-shop-safety-officer-judgment` (plus a\n  generic `:override-safety-officer-judgment` variant and matching\n  phrase variants, to defend against either phrasing appearing in an\n  advisor's free text) covers the specified scope without inventing an\n  unspecified second authority role.\n- Merging `worker` and `workshop` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered worker AND independently-registered\n  workshop) matches the CRITICAL invariant that a \"worker/workshop\n  record must be independently verified/registered before any action\"\n  and the established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors\n  with two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- task/batch/materials-usage/progress data\n   logging only. Requires an independently registered worker AND\n   workshop (`:no-worker`/`:no-workshop`). Not a machine-setup or\n   machining-execution decision in any respect -- it is a\n   records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task/machine-time scheduling\n   proposal only. Cannot carry a machine-setup or machining-execution\n   decision (structurally absent from the op's field shape; any such\n   attempt via free text is caught by the `:scope-excluded-action`\n   content check). Does not authorize, finalize, or draw any\n   conclusion about a specific lathe, mill or press setup/run -- it is\n   a calendar/logistics action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a machinery-hazard/\n   tool-condition concern to a human/shop safety officer; ALWAYS\n   escalates, unconditionally, at every confidence level, NEVER\n   auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY channel through which a\n   safety observation may be surfaced, and it explicitly never\n   resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself -- the shop safety\n   officer reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- tooling-materials procurement\n   proposal only (item, cost). Not a machine-setup, machining-execution\n   or authorization decision of any kind -- it is a\n   purchasing-logistics action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling\n   escalation (above 2000 always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na machine-setup or machining-execution decision (e.g. proceeding with a\nspecific lathe, mill or press setup/run) or a\nshop-safety-officer-judgment override -- these categories are\nstructurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op\nallowlist itself (`machinist.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) five\nconcretely-named forbidden-op fixtures rejected at the\ngovernor-unit level (`machinist.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`machinist.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-machine-setup-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-machining-operation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-machine-setup`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`),\nand (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`machinist.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to proceed with a machine setup or override a shop\nsafety officer's judgment even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`machinist.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-shop-safety-officer`).\n"), (:adr/date "2026-07-18"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 2000}"), (:test/counts "{:total 22, :assertions 47, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Metal Working Machine Tool Coordination Practice"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :worker-verification :workshop-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-7223"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7223"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7223)), (:test/simulator :machinist.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-7223 — Metal Working Machine Tool Setters and Operators Workshop Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-7223)))
